Instruction: Read the excerpt from a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the clause regarding the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions subject to the carveouts set forth in the definition of 'Transaction Impact'?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt: 
"'Transaction Impact' shall mean any deviation, event, occurrence, or development that, either individually or in aggregate, (a) has a material adverse effect on the operational, financial, or business conditions of the Company, or (b) would prevent, materially delay, or otherwise impair the Company’s ability to complete the contemplated transactions. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for the purposes of clause (b) only, the following shall not be deemed as contributing to a Transaction Impact: 
   (i) variations solely attributable to general market fluctuations or macroeconomic trends affecting all industry participants; 
   (ii) normal fluctuations in interest rates and currency exchange rates as published by recognized financial authorities; 
   (iii) changes in regulatory or statutory frameworks that broadly impact the entire sector; 
   (iv) temporary operational interruptions due to force majeure events, provided such events do not impose a disproportionate burden on the Company relative to its competitors; and 
   (v) revisions to accounting standards or market sentiment shifts that result in non-material, short-term impacts."

